// @run
// @log 3
// @log y
// @log 2.5
// @log 7
// @log z
// A TOP-LEVEL INFERRED EMPTY `[]` WHOSE ELEMENT A `.push` PINS.
//
// The nested-ARRAY element used to check clean and emit a module that does not instantiate —
// the global cell took the i32-list default while the value pushed into it was a list ref.
// Graduated from `soundness/xfail-miscompile-toplevel-nested-array-inferred-empty.vl`. The
// IN-FUNCTION form of the same three leaf kinds is `nested-array-inferred-empty-push.vl`
// beside this file; the last two arms are the flat scalar/string elements, which worked at
// top level before and are here so a regression shows up in the same file.
//
// THE SYNTHESIS EXISTED AND THE STORAGE CLASS COULD NOT REACH IT. `collectLocals` walks a
// FUNCTION BODY's block statements to type wasm LOCALS, and it opens by synthesizing the
// `<elem>[]` annotation of an un-annotated empty `[]` from the checker's recorded type — the
// pin `.push` produced. A top-level binding is a GLOBAL and never enters that walk, so the
// annotation was never synthesized and `globalCellKind`'s un-annotated arm guessed.
//
// ONE HOME, TWO CALLERS, which is the shape of the fix: the arm is now
// `synthEmptyListAnn(letIx)` and a module-scope pass runs it over `globalStmts`. Copying it
// would have left the two scopes one element kind apart at the next widening, which is
// exactly what `declareForInLocals`' header records happening to the for-in loop's slots.
//
// ORDERING IS A REQUIREMENT, not a convenience: the pass runs after `collectA#2`, because the
// synthesis can only RESOLVE a ref-list row and never mint one (a late intern would append
// past the resolved heap tables), and before `emitModule`, whose global section reads the
// annotated ladder this steers.
//
// WHY THIS WAS THE ONLY MISCOMPILING ELEMENT KIND at top level, and what to check if the
// pass ever regresses: the others rejected LOUDLY — a struct element with `field access
// receiver is not a struct`, a union element with ``is`` receiver is not a union value``, a
// closure element with `callee is not a function name`. A clean rejection is safe; this one
// was not, which is the whole reason the xfail existed.
const oi = []
const ii = [1, 2, 3]
oi.push(ii)
print(oi[0][2])

const os = []
const is2 = ["x", "y"]
os.push(is2)
print(os[0][1])

const of2 = []
const if2 = [1.5, 2.5]
of2.push(if2)
print(of2[0][1])

const flat = []
flat.push(7)
print(flat[0])

const fstr = []
fstr.push("z")
print(fstr[0])
